    id: 127,
    title: "Lumpia Shanghai",
    description:
      "Crispy Filipino spring rolls filled with seasoned ground pork and vegetables, served with sweet chilli sauce.",
    difficulty: "medium",
    image: "",
    content:
      "Lumpia Shanghai are slim, crackly Filipino spring rolls packed with garlicky pork filling – a fixture at every party and fiesta. Rolling them thin is the secret to a filling that cooks through as the wrapper turns golden.\n\n### Ingredients:\n\n* 500 g ground pork\n* 1 medium carrot (100 g), finely grated\n* 1 small onion (80 g), finely chopped\n* 4 cloves garlic, minced\n* 2 spring onions, finely sliced\n* 50 g water chestnuts or jicama, finely chopped\n* 1 large egg\n* 1 tbsp (15 ml) soy sauce\n* 1 tsp (5 ml) fish sauce (patis)\n* 1 tsp (5 g) salt\n* 1/2 tsp (1 g) ground black pepper\n* 30 spring roll (lumpia) wrappers, about 20 cm square, thawed\n* 1 tbsp (15 ml) water, for sealing\n* 1 l neutral oil, for frying\n* 200 ml sweet chilli sauce, to serve\n\n### Instructions:\n\n1. In a large bowl combine the pork, carrot, onion, garlic, spring onions, water chestnuts, egg, soy sauce, fish sauce, salt and pepper. Mix with your hands for 1 minute until sticky and evenly combined.\n2. Fry a teaspoon of filling in a little oil for 2 minutes, taste and adjust seasoning in the main bowl.\n3. Separate the wrappers and keep them under a damp tea towel so they don't dry out.\n4. Lay a wrapper on the board as a diamond. Spread about 1 heaped tbsp (20 g) of filling in a thin log, about 1.5 cm thick, along the bottom edge, leaving 2 cm free at each side.\n5. Fold the bottom corner over the filling, fold in the sides and roll up tightly. Brush the top corner with water and press to seal. Repeat with the remaining filling.\n6. Cut each roll in half or thirds on the diagonal (about 7 cm pieces) if you like bite-size lumpia.\n7. Heat the oil in a 24 cm deep pan or wok to 170–175 °C.\n8. Fry the lumpia in batches of 6–8 for 5–6 minutes, turning, until deep golden and blistered; the filling should reach 71 °C.\n9. Drain on a rack set over kitchen paper and serve hot with sweet chilli sauce or banana ketchup.\n\n### Tips:\n\n* Freeze uncooked rolls on a tray, then bag them; fry straight from frozen at 165 °C for 8–9 minutes.\n* Keep the filling thin – fat rolls brown before the pork is cooked through.\n* Oil that is too cool makes greasy lumpia; fry in small batches to keep the temperature up.",
    servings: 6,
    slug: "lumpia-shanghai",
    time: 75,
    cuisines: [29], // Filipino
    categories: [7, 6, 22], // Appetisers, Sandwiches, Wraps and Rolls, Pork
