    title: "Beef Rendang",
    description:
      "A rich, dry Indonesian beef curry slowly braised in coconut milk, spices, and toasted coconut.",
    difficulty: "hard",
    image: "",
    content:
      "Beef rendang, from the Minangkabau of West Sumatra, is cooked for hours until the coconut milk reduces completely and the spices caramelise around the tender beef. The result is dark, intensely flavoured and almost dry.\n\n### Ingredients:\n\n**For the spice paste:**\n\n* 10 dried red chillies, soaked and seeded\n* 5 fresh red chillies\n* 8 shallots (160 g)\n* 6 cloves garlic\n* 4 cm piece galangal\n* 3 cm piece ginger\n* 3 cm piece fresh turmeric (or 1 tsp/2 g ground)\n* 3 candlenuts (or macadamias)\n\n**For the rendang:**\n\n* 1 kg beef chuck or brisket, cut into 5 cm chunks\n* 3 tbsp (45 ml) neutral oil\n* 2 lemongrass stalks, bruised and knotted\n* 4 kaffir lime leaves, torn\n* 1 turmeric leaf, knotted (optional)\n* 1 cinnamon stick (5 cm)\n* 3 cloves and 3 star anise\n* 4 cardamom pods, crushed\n* 800 ml coconut milk\n* 100 ml water\n* 60 g kerisik (toasted grated coconut, pounded)\n* 1 tbsp (15 g) tamarind paste\n* 1 tbsp (12 g) palm sugar\n* 1 1/2 tsp (8 g) salt\n\n### Instructions:\n\n1. Blend the spice paste ingredients with 2–3 tbsp water until very smooth.\n2. Make the kerisik: toast 80 g desiccated coconut in a dry pan over medium-low heat for 8–10 minutes, stirring, until deep brown, then pound or blend until oily and paste-like.\n3. Heat the oil in a 28 cm heavy pot or wok over medium heat. Fry the paste for 8–10 minutes, stirring, until fragrant, darker and the oil separates.\n4. Add the lemongrass, lime leaves, turmeric leaf and whole spices and fry for 1 minute.\n5. Add the beef and stir to coat for 3–4 minutes, then pour in the coconut milk and water, and add the tamarind, sugar and salt.\n6. Bring to a boil, then lower the heat and simmer uncovered for 2 hours, stirring every 15 minutes as the liquid reduces.\n7. Stir in the kerisik and continue cooking for 1–1 1/2 hours, stirring more often as it thickens, until the sauce is almost dry, dark brown and the oil separates.\n8. In the final 20 minutes, stir constantly over low heat so the spices caramelise on the beef without burning; the beef should be fork-tender.\n9. Remove the lemongrass and whole spices, adjust the salt and serve with steamed rice.\n\n### Tips:\n\n* Rendang keeps for a week in the fridge and improves daily; it also freezes for 3 months.\n* Don't rush the final stage – the dark, dry finish is what defines rendang versus a curry.\n* Short rib or beef shin also work well and stay very moist.",
