    title: "Bún Chả",
    description:
      "Hanoi's grilled pork patties and pork belly in a sweet-sour dipping broth with rice noodles and herbs.",
    difficulty: "medium",
    image: "",
    content:
      "Bún chả is Hanoi's iconic lunch: smoky grilled pork served in warm nước chấm with cold rice vermicelli and a pile of fresh herbs. Charcoal grilling gives the most authentic flavour.\n\n### Ingredients:\n\n**For the pork:**\n\n* 400 g pork shoulder mince (20–30% fat)\n* 400 g pork belly, sliced 3 mm thick\n* 4 shallots (80 g), minced\n* 4 cloves garlic, minced\n* 3 tbsp (45 ml) fish sauce\n* 2 tbsp (25 g) sugar\n* 1 tbsp (15 ml) caramel sauce (nước màu) or 1 tsp dark soy\n* 1 tbsp (15 ml) neutral oil\n* 1 tsp (2 g) ground black pepper\n\n**For the dipping sauce:**\n\n* 500 ml warm water\n* 80 ml fish sauce\n* 70 g sugar\n* 60 ml rice vinegar or lime juice\n* 2 cloves garlic, minced\n* 2 bird's eye chillies, sliced\n* 1 small carrot and 100 g green papaya, thinly sliced\n\n**To serve:**\n\n* 400 g dried rice vermicelli\n* 1 lettuce, leaves separated\n* Large bunch of herbs: mint, perilla, Vietnamese balm, coriander\n\n### Instructions:\n\n1. Mix the shallots, garlic, fish sauce, sugar, caramel, oil and pepper. Divide between the mince and the sliced belly and mix well.\n2. Shape the mince into 16 small patties, about 5 cm wide and 1.5 cm thick. Cover both meats and marinate in the fridge for 1 hour.\n3. For the dipping sauce, dissolve the sugar in the warm water, then add the fish sauce, vinegar, garlic and chillies. Add the carrot and papaya and set aside.\n4. Cook the vermicelli in boiling water for 4–5 minutes, rinse under cold water, drain and leave to dry in small nests.\n5. Heat a charcoal grill or grill pan to high heat. Grill the belly slices in a grill basket for 3–4 minutes per side until charred and caramelised.\n6. Grill the patties for 3–4 minutes per side until charred at the edges and cooked through (71 °C).\n7. Warm the dipping sauce slightly, divide into 4 bowls and add the hot grilled pork to each.\n8. Serve with the noodles, lettuce and herbs on the side; diners dip noodles and herbs into the bowl of pork and broth.\n\n### Tips:\n\n* The pork can marinate overnight for deeper flavour.\n* Without a grill, use an oven grill at 250 °C with the rack 8–10 cm from the element.\n* Balance the dipping sauce to taste – it should be sweet, salty and tangy in equal measure.",
