    difficulty: "easy",
    image: "",
    content:
      "Cachapas are thick, rustic pancakes made from fresh sweetcorn, sold at roadside stands across Venezuela. They are folded over melting queso de mano and served with butter.\n\n### Ingredients:\n\n* 600 g fresh sweetcorn kernels (about 4–5 cobs) or frozen, thawed\n* 120 ml whole milk\n* 1 large egg\n* 60 g fine yellow cornmeal (precooked harina de maíz, e.g. P.A.N.)\n* 2 tbsp (25 g) sugar\n* 1/2 tsp (3 g) salt\n* 30 g unsalted butter, melted, plus extra for the griddle\n* 400 g queso de mano or fresh mozzarella, sliced (or queso telita)\n\n### Instructions:\n\n1. Put the corn, milk, egg, sugar, salt and melted butter in a blender and blend to a thick, slightly coarse batter.\n2. Add the cornmeal and blend briefly to combine. The batter should be like thick pancake batter; add more cornmeal if very runny.\n3. Rest the batter for 10 minutes to let the cornmeal hydrate.\n4. Heat a 24 cm non-stick pan, cast-iron griddle or budare over medium heat and brush lightly with butter.\n5. Pour in about 120 ml batter and spread into a 15 cm round about 1 cm thick.\n6. Cook for 3–4 minutes until the edges are set, the surface is mostly dry and the bottom is golden brown with darker spots.\n7. Flip carefully and cook for a further 2–3 minutes until golden.\n8. Place slices of cheese on half the cachapa, fold it over and let the cheese soften for 1 minute. Serve with butter.\n\n### Tips:\n\n* Sweet, starchy fresh corn gives the best flavour; with frozen corn, add an extra tbsp of sugar.\n* Keep the heat moderate – the sugar in the corn burns easily.\n* Leftover batter keeps in the fridge for 1 day; stir before using.",
