    id: 152,
    title: "Kotlet Schabowy",
    description:
      "A crisp, golden breaded pork loin cutlet pan-fried in lard, Poland's most beloved Sunday dinner dish.",
    difficulty: "easy",
    image: "",
    content:
      "Kotlet schabowy is the cornerstone of the Polish obiad: a pounded boneless pork loin chop coated in flour, egg and breadcrumbs, then fried until shatteringly crisp. It is traditionally served with potatoes and a cabbage or cucumber salad.\n\n### Ingredients:\n\n* 4 boneless pork loin chops (about 150 g each, 1.5 cm thick)\n* 1 tsp (6 g) salt\n* ½ tsp (1 g) freshly ground black pepper\n* 50 g plain flour\n* 2 large eggs\n* 2 tbsp (30 ml) milk\n* 100 g fine dried breadcrumbs\n* 150 g lard (or 180 ml sunflower oil)\n* 1 tbsp (15 g) butter\n* 1 lemon, cut into wedges, to serve\n\n### Instructions:\n\n1. Place each chop between two sheets of cling film and pound with the flat side of a meat mallet to an even 0.5–0.7 cm thickness, working from the centre outwards. Snip the fat edge in 2–3 places so it doesn't curl.\n2. Season both sides with salt and pepper.\n3. Set up three shallow plates: flour; eggs beaten with the milk and a pinch of salt; breadcrumbs.\n4. Dredge each cutlet in flour and shake off the excess, dip in egg letting the surplus drip off, then press firmly into the breadcrumbs on both sides. Rest on a rack for 5 minutes so the coating sets.\n5. Heat the lard and butter in a 28 cm heavy frying pan over medium-high heat to about 170 °C; a breadcrumb dropped in should sizzle immediately. The fat should be about 0.5 cm deep.\n6. Fry 2 cutlets at a time for 3–4 minutes per side until deep golden brown and the juices run clear (internal temperature 63–65 °C). Do not move them during the first minute.\n7. Drain on a rack or paper towels and keep warm in a 100 °C oven while frying the rest.\n8. Serve immediately with lemon wedges.\n\n### Tips:\n\n* Serve the Polish way with boiled new potatoes sprinkled with dill and butter, and mizeria (cucumbers in sour cream) or braised cabbage.\n* Lard gives the most authentic flavour and crispest crust; clarified butter is a good alternative.\n* Avoid pounding too thin or the meat will dry out before the crust browns.\n* Leftovers keep 2 days refrigerated; reheat in a 200 °C oven for 8–10 minutes to recrisp.",
    servings: 4,
    slug: "kotlet-schabowy",
