    id: 153,
    title: "Rosół",
    description:
      "A clear, golden Polish chicken and beef broth simmered slowly with root vegetables and served with thin noodles.",
    difficulty: "medium",
    image: "",
    content:
      "Rosół is the Polish Sunday soup, a crystal-clear broth that simmers for hours with chicken, beef and root vegetables. It is served piping hot over fine egg noodles with plenty of fresh parsley.\n\n### Ingredients:\n\n* 1 small whole chicken or boiling hen (about 1.2 kg), or 1 kg chicken legs and backs\n* 500 g beef shin or brisket on the bone\n* 3.5 l cold water\n* 3 carrots (about 300 g), peeled\n* 2 parsley roots (about 150 g), peeled\n* 150 g celeriac, peeled\n* 1 leek (white and light green part), washed\n* ¼ small savoy cabbage (about 150 g) (optional)\n* 1 large onion, halved, skin on\n* 4 allspice berries, 3 bay leaves, 8 black peppercorns\n* 1 tbsp (18 g) salt, plus more to taste\n* 1 small bunch flat-leaf parsley, chopped\n* 300 g thin egg noodles (nitki)\n\n### Instructions:\n\n1. Rinse the chicken and beef, place in a large 6 l pot and cover with the cold water. Bring slowly to a bare simmer over medium-low heat, about 30 minutes.\n2. Skim off all the grey foam as it rises during the first 15 minutes. Reduce the heat so the surface only trembles; never let it boil, or the broth will turn cloudy.\n3. Simmer uncovered or with the lid ajar for 1 hour 30 minutes.\n4. Meanwhile, char the onion halves cut-side down in a dry pan over high heat for 5–6 minutes until blackened; this gives rosół its golden colour.\n5. Add the carrots, parsley root, celeriac, leek, cabbage, charred onion, allspice, bay leaves, peppercorns and salt. Simmer gently for a further 1 hour 15 minutes, until the vegetables are tender and the meat falls from the bone.\n6. Lift out the meat and vegetables. Strain the broth through a sieve lined with a damp cloth. Taste and adjust the salt.\n7. Cook the noodles in salted water according to the packet (usually 2–3 minutes), drain and divide among bowls.\n8. Slice the carrots into rounds. Ladle the hot broth over the noodles, add carrot slices and sprinkle generously with parsley.\n\n### Tips:\n\n* Serve the Polish way with nitki noodles and carrot; the leftover meat is often eaten alongside or minced into pierogi or pasztet.\n* Leftover rosół traditionally becomes zupa pomidorowa (tomato soup) the next day.\n* Chill the broth overnight to lift off the solidified fat easily; it keeps 4 days in the fridge or 3 months frozen.\n* Cook the noodles separately, never in the broth, so it stays clear.",
