    id: 154,
    title: "Placki Ziemniaczane",
    description:
      "Crispy Polish potato pancakes made from grated potatoes and onion, fried golden and served with sour cream.",
    difficulty: "easy",
    image: "",
    content:
      "Placki ziemniaczane are thin, lacy potato pancakes with crisp edges and a soft centre. In Poland they are eaten with sour cream, a sprinkle of sugar, or topped with goulash as placek po węgiersku.\n\n### Ingredients:\n\n* 1 kg floury potatoes, peeled\n* 1 medium onion (about 120 g), peeled\n* 2 large eggs\n* 3 tbsp (30 g) plain flour or potato starch\n* 1 tsp (6 g) salt\n* ¼ tsp (0.5 g) freshly ground black pepper\n* ½ tsp (1 g) dried marjoram (optional)\n* 150 ml sunflower or rapeseed oil, for frying\n* 250 ml sour cream 18%, to serve\n\n### Instructions:\n\n1. Grate the potatoes and onion on the finest side of a box grater (or with a food processor's fine grating disc) into a large bowl. Grating the onion with the potatoes helps stop them going grey.\n2. Tip into a sieve set over a bowl and press firmly to squeeze out the liquid. Leave the liquid for 5 minutes, then pour it off, keeping the white potato starch that settles at the bottom.\n3. Return the potatoes to the bowl with the reserved starch. Add the eggs, flour, salt, pepper and marjoram and mix well. The batter should be thick but loose.\n4. Heat about 0.5 cm of oil in a 28 cm heavy frying pan over medium-high heat to about 180 °C, until a drop of batter sizzles vigorously.\n5. Drop heaped tablespoons (about 50 g) of batter into the pan and flatten each into a thin 8–10 cm round with the back of the spoon. Fit 4–5 per batch.\n6. Fry for 3–4 minutes per side until deep golden and crisp at the edges. Top up the oil between batches and let it reheat.\n7. Drain on a rack or paper towels and keep warm in a 100 °C oven until all are fried.\n8. Serve hot with sour cream.\n\n### Tips:\n\n* Serve the Polish way with sour cream and a pinch of salt, or with sugar for a sweet version; for placek po węgiersku, fold one large pancake over hot goulash.\n* Make the batter just before frying; it darkens and turns watery if left standing.\n* Too cool oil makes greasy pancakes, so wait for it to reheat between batches.\n* Leftovers reheat well in a dry pan or a 200 °C oven for 5–6 minutes; keep 1–2 days refrigerated.",
    servings: 4,
    slug: "placki-ziemniaczane",
    time: 45,
    cuisines: [23], // Polish
    categories: [10, 11], // Sides, Snacks
