    id: 155,
    title: "Sernik",
    description:
      "A rich, creamy Polish baked cheesecake made with twaróg curd cheese on a buttery shortcrust base.",
    difficulty: "medium",
    image: "",
    content:
      "Sernik is Poland's favourite cake, made with twaróg, a tangy fresh curd cheese that gives a lighter, slightly crumbly texture than cream-cheese cheesecakes. This version is baked on a shortcrust base and cooled slowly to keep the top smooth.\n\n### Ingredients:\n\n**For the base:**\n\n* 200 g plain flour\n* 100 g cold butter, cubed\n* 60 g icing sugar\n* 1 egg yolk\n* 1 tbsp (15 ml) cold sour cream\n* 1 tsp (4 g) baking powder\n\n**For the filling:**\n\n* 1 kg full-fat twaróg (twaróg sernikowy, ground)\n* 150 g butter, softened\n* 200 g caster sugar\n* 5 large eggs, separated\n* 2 tbsp (16 g) vanilla sugar or 1 tsp (5 ml) vanilla extract\n* 40 g potato starch\n* Finely grated zest of 1 lemon\n* 100 g raisins (optional)\n\n### Instructions:\n\n1. For the base, rub the flour, baking powder and butter together until sandy, then mix in the icing sugar, yolk and sour cream to form a dough. Wrap and chill for 30 minutes.\n2. Heat the oven to 180 °C (conventional). Line the bottom of a 24 cm springform tin with baking paper and grease the sides.\n3. Press the dough evenly over the base of the tin, prick with a fork and bake for 12–15 minutes until pale golden. Lower the oven to 160 °C.\n4. If your twaróg is not pre-ground, press it twice through a fine sieve or mincer for a smooth texture. Ensure all filling ingredients are at room temperature.\n5. Beat the butter with the sugar and vanilla sugar for 3–4 minutes until pale. Beat in the yolks one at a time, then add the twaróg in portions, mixing on low speed until smooth. Mix in the starch, lemon zest and raisins.\n6. Whisk the egg whites with a pinch of salt to stiff, glossy peaks. Fold them into the cheese mixture in three additions with a spatula, keeping as much air as possible.\n7. Pour the filling over the base and smooth the top. Bake at 160 °C for 60–70 minutes, until the top is lightly golden and the centre wobbles only slightly when the tin is nudged.\n8. Turn off the oven, prop the door open with a wooden spoon and leave the sernik inside for 1 hour to cool slowly; this prevents cracking and sinking.\n9. Remove, run a knife around the edge and cool completely at room temperature, then chill for at least 1 hour (ideally overnight) before slicing.\n\n### Tips:\n\n* Twaróg substitution: use 1 kg ricotta drained overnight, or 800 g full-fat quark plus 200 g cream cheese; avoid low-fat cheese, which makes the filling watery.\n* Serve the Polish way in generous squares with coffee or tea, dusted with icing sugar or glazed with melted dark chocolate.\n* Overbeating after adding the twaróg incorporates too much air and causes cracks.\n* Keep refrigerated, covered, for up to 5 days; the flavour improves on day two.",
    servings: 12,
    slug: "sernik",
    time: 270,
    cuisines: [23], // Polish
    categories: [15, 30], // Desserts, Cakes
